/**
  @page guides-meshes-utilities Mesh operations
  @brief Change coordinates, extract submeshes and group cells.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-meshes-utilities-intro Choose the operation

  Coordinate changes modify the geometric map. Extraction selects entities
  into a submesh. Component labeling and partitioning assign groups to the
  existing cells. Establish the required connectivity before each operation
  and retain a parent mesh while a submesh refers to it.

  @section guides-meshes-utilities-transform Coordinate transformations

  @subsection guides-meshes-utilities-transform-scale Scaling

  `mesh.scale(a)` multiplies coordinates by `a`. Length, area and volume
  consequently scale by @f$|a|@f$, @f$a^2@f$ and @f$|a|^3@f$. A grid with
  16 vertices per axis initially spans zero to 15; scaling by two gives side
  length 30. Normalize by @f$1/15@f$ first when a unit-square baseline is needed.

  @subsection guides-meshes-utilities-transform-displace Displacement

  `mesh.displace(displacement)` evaluates a vector-valued displacement on the
  mesh geometry and updates the coordinates. Supply one component for each
  embedding-space coordinate. The small
  @ref Geometry/Displace.cpp "Displace.cpp example" writes its original and
  deformed meshes. Changes to geometry must preserve the cell regularity
  required by subsequent integration and solution procedures.

  @section guides-meshes-utilities-submesh Extract a submesh

  @subsection guides-meshes-utilities-submesh-create Keep a region

  `mesh.keep(attribute)` extracts cells carrying the requested label. The
  result is a `SubMesh` with parent associations. `getParent()` gives access
  to the parent mesh; use the submesh mappings when corresponding indices
  between the two are needed.

  @subsection guides-meshes-utilities-submesh-boundary Extract the boundary

  `mesh.skin()` extracts boundary facets after facet-to-cell connectivity
  has been prepared. A planar rectangle gives a one-dimensional curve.

  @section guides-meshes-utilities-ccl Connected components

  `mesh.ccl(predicate)` groups adjacent cells accepted by the binary predicate.
  `getComponents()` returns those groups as collections of cell indices.
  In the example, equality of cell attributes gives one component for each
  of two connected regions.

  @snippet Geometry/MeshBasics.cpp mesh-basics-extract

  `trace()` assigns facet attributes from neighboring region labels. Region
  extraction uses `keep()`; the @ref guides-interfaces-dg "interface guide"
  describes the trace-labeling operation.

  @section guides-meshes-utilities-partition Partition cell indices

  @subsection guides-meshes-utilities-partition-balanced Balanced partitioning

  Construct the partitioner with the mesh, call `partition(count)` and query
  the resulting partition for each cell index. The mesh and its prepared
  adjacency remain alive during this procedure.

  @snippet Geometry/MeshBasics.cpp mesh-basics-partition

  The partition labels can be used by later distribution operations.
  @ref guides-mpi "MPI mesh distribution" also establishes rank ownership,
  ghosts and communication state.

  @subsection guides-meshes-utilities-partition-greedy Greedy partitioning

  `GreedyPartitioner` provides another partitioning policy. Consult its
  constructor and partition interface in the API reference when selecting
  that policy for an application.

  @section guides-meshes-utilities-coord Direct coordinate edits

  @subsection guides-meshes-utilities-coord-set Set coordinates

  `setVertexCoordinates(index, value, component)` changes one coordinate;
  the vector overload changes the complete coordinate tuple. Coordinate
  edits affect any geometric quantities derived from the edited vertices.

  @section guides-meshes-utilities-flush Geometry cache lifetime

  `mesh.flush()` clears the transformation and quadrature indices. Subsequent
  geometry queries construct default maps from vertex coordinates. Retain and
  reinstall the intended higher-order transformations when preserving curved
  geometry through an explicit flush.
  Follow the chosen mutation API's cache contract. Reacquire borrowed geometry
  after invalidation, and rebuild spaces and algebraic problems after topology
  changes. See @ref guides-numerical-contracts.

  @section guides-meshes-utilities-example Checked source

  The `MeshBasics` target checks region extraction, boundary extraction,
  component counts and analytic areas, and exercises balanced partitioning.
  Its complete source is @ref Geometry/MeshBasics.cpp.

  @section guides-meshes-utilities-advanced Lower-level control

  @subsection guides-meshes-utilities-advanced-builder Builder

  The @ref guides-meshes-creation "creation guide" explains explicit vertex,
  cell and attribute construction. Builder reservation can reduce repeated
  allocation when the expected entity counts are known.

  @subsection guides-meshes-utilities-advanced-trans Transformations

  Mesh polytopes provide their geometric transformations. A
  @ref Rodin::Geometry::Point "Point" is the usual interface for evaluating
  physical coordinates and Jacobians at reference coordinates.

  @section guides-meshes-utilities-see-also Continue

  - @ref guides-meshes-queries "Geometric measures"
  - @ref guides-meshes-connectivity "Connectivity prerequisites"
  - @ref guides-mpi "Distributed meshes"

  @example Geometry/Displace.cpp
*/
